\par }\page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} AGC}} {\cs16\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} AGC}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} AGC}} {\fs44 AGC
\par }{\f5 
\par   Automatic Gain Control: The intention of automatic gain control is to automatically adjust the gain of a receiver to compensate for variations in received signal 
strength. In AM this is usually quite simple, a circuit monitoring the received carrier strength can quickly adjust the receiver gain and a fast AGC is usually used. For single sideband, AGC is far more complex. An ideal single sideband AGC system must se
t the AGC rapidly when a new signal appears and follow a rising or fading signal but, if the signal disappears altogether (as in pauses in speech), retain the AGC level until the signal recommences. If the signal remains absent for more than a preset time
, 
however, the system should rapidly revert to full gain. The DSP 100 has three AGC settings OFF, ON, and HOLD. In the OFF position no AGC is applied. With the AGC in the HOLD position the DSP 100 runs an algorithm to perform the ideal single sideband AGC s
ystem above.   The sound cards used with the DSP100 have a low frequency response of about 5 Hz. Therefore when an AM signal is locked in synchronous detection there is no carrier to measure. For this reason the DSP100 used a form of SSB AGC for AM recept
ion in the ON position. 
\par 
\par See also:
\par }\pard \fi-720\li720 {\f5     }{\f5\uldb Changing AGC}{\v\f5 Changing_AGC}{\f5 
\par }\pard {\f5     }{\f5\uldb Knobs}{\v\f5 Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Amateur_Bands}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Amateur Bands}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Amateur Bands}} {\fs44 Amateur Bands Window}{
\fs32 
\par }\pard 
\par {\f5   The Amateur Bands Window is a collection of the 9 amateur bands in the HF spectrum. This window allows for easy change from one band to another.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Changing Bands}{\v\f5 Changing_Bands}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Analog_Gain}} {\cs16\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Analog_Gain}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K}
 Analog_Gain}} {\fs44 Analog Gain
\par }\pard {\f5 
\par   The DSP100 has a programmable analog amplifiers with a max gain of 22.5dB and a step size of 1.5 dB 
prior to both the I and Q analog to digital converters. The analog gain controls these amplifiers. Although these amplifiers are not IF amplifiers this control is roughly equivalent to the IF gain on a conventional receiver. 
\par 
\par See also:
\par }\pard \fi-720\li720 {\f5     }{\f5\uldb Changing Analog Gain}{\v\f5 Changing_Analog_Gain}{\f5 
\par }\pard {\f5     }{\f5\uldb Knobs}{\v\f5 Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Band}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Bands}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Bands}} {\fs44 Bands}{\fs32 
\par }\pard 
\par {\f5   Bands are limits placed on the frequency range that }{\f5\uldb VFO A}{\v\f5 VFO_A}{\f5  and }{\f5\uldb VFO B}{\v\f5 VFO_B}{\f5  are allowed to tune. These bands may be either preset or can be adjusted by the user. The current b
and limits can be shown by displaying the }{\f5\uldb Minimum Frequency}{\v\f5 Minimum_Frequency}{\f5  and }{\f5\uldb Maximum Frequency}{\v\f5 Maximum_Frequency}{\f5  windows.
\par 
\par See Also
\par     }{\f5\uldb Changing Bands}{\v\f5 Changing_Bands}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb VFO A}{\v\f5 VFO_A}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb VFO B}{\v\f5 VFO_B}{\f5 
\par ....}{\f5\uldb Minimum Frequency}{\v\f5 Minimum_Frequency}{\f5 
\par ....}{\f5\uldb Maximum Frequency}{\v\f5 Maximum_Frequency}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Callsign}} {\cs16\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Callsign}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Callsign}} {
\fs44 Callsign Window
\par }\pard {\f5 
\par   The callsign window displays the users callsign. Although the main use of this window is just to show off your callsign, this information is used when calling CQ via the }{\f5\uldb Morse Code Macros}{\v\f5 Morse_Code_Macros}{\f5 
. It is also used to determine if you are allowed to transmit. An invalid callsign in this window will cause an error when you attempt to switch }{\f5\uldb PTT}{\v\f5 PTT}{\f5  to transmit.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Changing Callsign}{\v\f5 Changing_Callsign}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Morse Code Macros}{\v\f5 Morse_Code_Macros}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb PTT}{\v\f5 PTT}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_AGC}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing AGC}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Changing AGC}} {\fs44 Changing AGC}
\par \pard {\f5 
\par   There are 2 options for changing }{\f5\uldb AGC}{\v\f5 AGC}{\f5 .
\par 
\par 
\par   Option 1: Move the mouse cursor to the center of the knob and click either the right of left mouse button. the right mouse button rotates the knob clockwise and the left mouse button turns the knob counterclockwise.
\par 
\par   Option 2: Clicking on any of the three buttons (OFF,ON or HOLD). with the right mouse button will select that choice.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par   }{\f5\uldb AGC}{\v\f5 AGC}{\f5 
\par   }{\f5\uldb Turning Knobs}{\v\f5 Turning_Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_Analog_Gain}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing Analog Gain}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Changing Analog Gain}} {\fs44 
Changing Analog Gain}
\par \pard {\f5 
\par   There are 2 options for changing the }{\f5\uldb Analog gain}{\v\f5 Analog_Gain}{\f5 .
\par 
\par 
\par   Option 1: Move the mouse cursor to the center of the knob and click either the right of left mouse button. the right mouse button increases the analog gain and the left mouse button decreases analog gain.
\par 
\par   Option 2: Clicking the right mouse button while the mouse cursor is in the analog gain window, but outside of the picture of the knob will cause the knob to turn to point the cursor. 9:00 is 0 gain, 12:00 is 10.5 db gain and 3:00 is 22.5 db gain.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par   }{\f5\uldb Analog Gain}{\v\f5 Analog_Gain}{\f5 
\par   }{\f5\uldb Turning Knobs}{\v\f5 Turning_Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc {\f5 \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_Bands}} {\cs16\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing Bands}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super 
K} Changing Bands}} {\fs44 Changing Bands
\par }\pard {\f5 
\par   There are 4 options for changing the }{\f5\uldb Band}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5 . If the current frequency of either VFO is out of range of a new }{\f5\uldb Band}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5 
, it will automatically be relocated to the nearest frequency that is within the select }{\f5\uldb Band}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5  limits.
\par 
\par }  {\f5 Option 1: Choose a }{\f5\uldb Band}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5  by selecting one from the bands menu.
\par 
\par   Option 2: All of the amateur bands are collected together in the }{\f5\uldb Amateur Bands}{\v\f5 Amateur_Bands}{\f5  Window. Clicking the left mouse button on any of these bands will select that band.
\par }
\par   {\f5 Option 3: You can tune the }{\f5\uldb Minimum Frequency}{\v\f5 Minimum_Frequency}{\f5  & }{\f5\uldb Maximum Frequency}{\v\f5 Maximum_Frequency}{\f5 
\par }
\par {\f5   Option 4:  Selecting All from one of the following menu items: Bands|Amateur, Bands|Aeronautical, Bands|Broadcast, or Bands|Marine, will place you in the lowest }{\f5\uldb Band}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5 
 of that catagory. To show that you are in an all bands mode, the frequency displays will turn blue. Until such time as you select another band or tune the }{\f5\uldb Minimum Frequency}{\v\f5 Minimum_Frequency}{\f5  or }{\f5\uldb Maximum Frequency}{\v\f5 
Maximum_Frequency}{\f5 , anytime you exceed the range of the current }{\f5\uldb Band}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5 , the next }{\f5\uldb Band}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5  will be selected.
\par }
\par 
\par {\f5 See Also:
\par ....}{\f5\uldb Bands}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5 
\par ....}{\f5\uldb Amateur Bands}{\v\f5 Amateur_Bands}{\f5 
\par ....}{\f5\uldb Minimum Frequency}{\v\f5 Minimum_Frequency}{\f5 
\par ....}{\f5\uldb Maximum Frequency}{\v\f5 Maximum_Frequency}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc {\f5 \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_Callsign}${\footnote \pard\plain \fi-720\li720 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing Callsign}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Changing Callsign
}} {\fs44 Changing Callsign}
\par \pard {\f5 
\par   There are 2 options for changing your }{\f5\uldb Callsign}{\v\f5 Callsign}{\f5 .
\par 
\par   Option 1: left click on the callsign in the }{\f5\uldb Callsign}{\v\f5 Callsign}{\f5  window. This will bring up a dialog box for you to enter your callsign in.
\par 
\par   Option 2: Open the }{\f5\uldb Configure}{\v\f5 Configure}{\f5  dialog from the menu. Then left click on the text field next to the word callsign. Type in your callsign.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Callsign}{\v\f5 Callsign}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Configure}{\v\f5 Configure}{\f5 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_Compression}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing Compression}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Changing Compression}} {\fs44 
Changing Compression}
\par \pard {\f5 
\par   There are 2 options for changing }{\f5\uldb Compression}{\v\f5 Compression}{\f5 
\par 
\par 
\par   Option 1: Move the mouse cursor to the center of the knob and click either the right of left mouse button. the right mouse button rotates the knob clockwise and the left mouse button turns the knob counterclockwise.
\par 
\par   Option 2: Clicking on either button (ON or OFF). with the right mouse button will select that choice.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par   }{\f5\uldb Compression}{\v\f5 Compression}{\f5 
\par   }{\f5\uldb Turning Knobs}{\v\f5 Turning_Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc {\f5 \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_Compression_Level}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing Compression Level}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K}
 Changing Compression Level}} {\fs44 Changing Compression Level}
\par \pard {\f5 
\par   There are 2 options for changing the }{\f5\uldb Compression Level}{\v\f5 Compression_Level}{\f5 
\par 
\par 
\par   Option 1: Move the mouse cursor to the center of the knob and click either the right of left mouse button. the right mouse button increases the compression level and the left mouse button decreases compression level.
\par 
\par   Option 2: Clicking the right mouse button while the mouse cursor is in the mic gain window, but outside of the picture of the knob will cause the knob to turn to point the cursor. 9:00 is minimum compression, and 3:00 is maximum compression. 3:01 throug
h 8:59 are ignored.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par   }{\f5\uldb Compression Level}{\v\f5 Compression_Level}{\f5 
\par   }{\f5\uldb Turning Knobs}{\v\f5 Turning_Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc {\f5 \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_Filters}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing Filters}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Changing Filters}} {\fs44 
Changing Filters}
\par \pard {\f5 
\par   There are 2 options for changing }{\f5\uldb Filters}{\v\f5 Filters}{\f5 
\par 
\par 
\par   Option 1: Move the mouse cursor to the center of the knob and click either the right of left mouse button. the right mouse button rotates the knob clockwise and the left mouse button turns the knob counterclockwise.
\par 
\par   Option 2: Clicking on a button (50,200,400,1800,2700,8K or 16K). with the right mouse button will select that choice. Note: 16K will only show up in AM while 8K will appear at all other times.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par   }{\f5\uldb Filters}{\v\f5 Filters}{\f5 
\par   }{\f5\uldb Turning Knobs}{\v\f5 Turning_Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_FM_Squelch}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing FM_Squelch}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Changing FM_Squelch}} {\fs44 
Changing FM Squelch}
\par \pard {\f5 
\par   There are 2 options for changing the }{\f5\uldb FM Squelch}{\v\f5 FM_Squelch}{\f5 .
\par 
\par 
\par   Option 1: Move the mouse cursor to the center of the knob and click either the right of left mouse button. the right mouse button increases the squelch and the left mouse button decreases squelch.
\par 
\par   Op
tion 2: Clicking the right mouse button while the mouse cursor is in the fm squelch window, but outside of the picture of the knob will cause the knob to turn to point the cursor. 9:00 is minimum gain, and 3:00 is maximum. The range from 3:01 to 8:59 is i
gnored.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par   }{\f5\uldb FM Squelch}{\v\f5 FM_Squelch}{\f5 
\par   }{\f5\uldb Turning Knobs}{\v\f5 Turning_Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc {\f5 \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_Maximum_Frequency}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing Maximum_Frequency}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K}
 Changing Maximum Frequency}} {\fs44 Changing Maximum Frequency}
\par \pard 
\par  {\f5  There are 4 options for tuning the Maximum Frequency. If the Maximum Frequency is tuned below the }{\f5\uldb Minimum Frequency}{\v\f5 Minimum_Frequency}{\f5 , the }{\f5\uldb Minimum Frequency}{\v\f5 Minimum_Frequency}{\f5 
 will be set to the Maximum Frequency. The same will happen for }{\f5\uldb VFO A}{\v\f5 VFO_A}{\f5  or }{\f5\uldb VFO B}{\v\f5 VFO_B}{\f5  whenever the maximum is tuned below them.
\par 
\par   Option 1: Move the mouse cursor over the digit you wish to tune. While  holding down the left mouse button, move the mouse left to tune down or right to tune up. Release the left mouse button when you have finished tuning. 
\par 
\par   Option 2: Move the cursor over the digit you wish to tune . Click the right mouse button. The cursor will disappear and the select digit will be highlighted. Moving the mouse will now change this digit, right will increase & left will decrease. To exit 
from this mode, click either mouse button.
\par 
\par   Option 3: Click on the button marked }{\f5\uldb Maximum Frequency}{\v\f5 Maximum_Frequency}{\f5 . This will bring up a }{\f5\uldb Number Pad}{\v\f5 Number_Pads}{\f5  on your display.
\par 
\par Option 4: Select a preset }{\f5\uldb Band}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5 
\par 
\par See Also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Band}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Maximum Frequency}{\v\f5 Maximum_Frequency}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Minimum Frequency}{\v\f5 Minimum_Frequency}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Number Pad}{\v\f5 Number_Pads}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc {\f5 \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_Mic_Gain}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing Mic Gain}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Changing Mic Gain}} {\fs44 
Changing Mic Gain}
\par \pard {\f5 
\par   There are 2 options for changing the }{\f5\uldb Mic gain}{\v\f5 Mic_Gain}{\f5 .
\par 
\par 
\par   Option 1: Move the mouse cursor to the center of the knob and click either the right of left mouse button. the right mouse button increases the mic gain and the left mouse button decreases mic gain.
\par 
\par   Option 2: Clicking the right mouse button while the mouse cursor is in the mic gain window, but outside of the picture of the knob will cause the knob to turn to point the cursor. 9:00 is minimum gain, and 3:00 is maximum gain. The range from 3:01 to 8:
59 is ignored.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par   }{\f5\uldb Mic Gain}{\v\f5 Mic_Gain}{\f5 
\par   }{\f5\uldb Turning Knobs}{\v\f5 Turning_Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc {\f5 \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_Minimum_Frequency}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing Minimum_Frequency}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K}
 Changing Minimum Frequency}} {\fs44 Changing Minimum Frequency}
\par \pard 
\par  {\f5  There are 4 options for tuning the Minimum Frequency.If the Minimum Frequency is tuned above the }{\f5\uldb Maximum Frequency}{\v\f5 Maximum_Frequency}{\f5 , the }{\f5\uldb Maximum Frequency}{\v\f5 Maximum_Frequency}{\f5 
 will be set to the Minimum Frequency. The same will happen for }{\f5\uldb VFO A}{\v\f5 VFO_A}{\f5  or }{\f5\uldb VFO B}{\v\f5 VFO_B}{\f5  whenever the minimum is tuned above them.
\par 
\par 
\par   Option 1: Move the mouse cursor over the digit you wish to tune. While  holding down the left mouse button, move the mouse left to tune down or right to tune up. Release the left mouse button when you have finished tuning. 
\par 
\par   Option 2: Move the cursor over the digit you wish to tune . Click the right mouse button. The cursor will disappear and the select digit will be highlighted. Moving the mouse will now change this digit, right will increase & left will decrease. To exit 
from this mode, click either mouse button.
\par 
\par   Option 3: Click on the button marked }{\f5\uldb Minimum Frequency}{\v\f5 Minimum_Frequency}{\f5 . This will bring up a }{\f5\uldb Number Pad}{\v\f5 Number_Pads}{\f5  on your display.
\par 
\par   Option 4: Select a preset }{\f5\uldb Band}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5 
\par 
\par See Also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Band}{\v\f5 Band}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Maximum Frequency}{\v\f5 Maximum_Frequency}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Minimum Frequency}{\v\f5 Minimum_Frequency}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Number Pad}{\v\f5 Number_Pads}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_Mixer_Gain}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing Mixer Gain}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Changing Mixer Gain}} {\fs44 
Changing Mixer Gain}
\par \pard {\f5 
\par   There are 2 options for changing }{\f5\uldb Mixer Gain}{\v\f5 Mixer_Gain}{\f5 .
\par 
\par 
\par   Option 1: Move the mouse cursor to the center of the knob and click either the right of left mouse button. the right mouse button rotates the knob clockwise and the left mouse button turns the knob counterclockwise.
\par 
\par   Option 2: Clicking on any of the three buttons (-10, 0, or 10 db). with the right mouse button will select that choice.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par   }{\f5\uldb Mixer Gain}{\v\f5 Mixer_Gain}{\f5 
\par   }{\f5\uldb Turning Knobs}{\v\f5 Turning_Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
