\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Changing_Volume}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Changing Volume}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Changing Volume}} {\fs44 Changing Volume}

\par \pard 
\par {\f5   There are  methods of changing the volume.
\par 
\par   Option 1: Right clicking in the center of the knob increase the volume. Left clicking in the center will decrease the volume.
\par 
\par   Option 2: Left clicking on the white background around the knob will jump the volume to the position clicked. 9 o\rquote clock is minimum, 12 o\rquote clock is middle and 3 o\rquote clock is maximum. 4 through 8 o\rquote clock are ignored.
\par 
\par   The volume control has 64 settings. As there are only 19 knob drawings, small increases in volume may not change the picture even though your radio has increased its volume.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Knobs}{\v\f5 Knobs}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Turning Knobs}{\v\f5 Turning_Knobs}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Volume}{\v\f5 Volume}{\f5 
\par 
\par }
\par \pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Compression}} {\cs16\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Compression}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K}
 Compression}} {\fs44 Compression
\par }\pard {\f5 
\par   The compression window allows you to choose whether or not compression will be on during transmit. Compression works by looking at each group of 256 samples and adjusting the gain so that the highest sample is at the }{\f5\uldb Compression Level}{\v\f5 
Compression_Level}{\f5 .
\par 
\par See also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Changing Compression}{\v\f5 Changing_Compression}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Compression Level}{\v\f5 Compression_Level}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Knobs}{\v\f5 Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Compression_Level}} {\cs16\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Compression Level}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super 
K} Compression Level}} {\fs44 Compression Level
\par }\pard {\f5 
\par   The compression level is the amplitude level to which the compression algorithm keeps the highest amplitude each 64th of a second.
\par 
\par See also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Changing Compression Level}{\v\f5 Changing_Compression_Level}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Compression}{\v\f5 Compression}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Knobs}{\v\f5 Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Filters}} {\cs16\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Filters}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} Filters}} {\fs44 
Filters
\par }\pard {\f5 
\par   There are 6 different available filters in the filters window. These filters are 50 Hz, 200 Hz, 400 Hz, 1800 Hz, 2700 Hz & either 8KHz or 16 KHz. The 50, 200, and 400 Hz filters all center on 1 KHz.  The 1800 Hz filter starts at 400 Hz and ends at 2200 
Hz The 2700 Hz filter starts at 300 Hz and ends at 3000 Hz. These filters are 81 tap DSP filters. The DSP 100 card also includes a *Brian Add* filter that rolls of sharply at 1/2 of the sample r
ate. This sample rate is 16 KHz for AM and 8 KHz for SSB. This filter is in at all times. The final setting in the filter window is either 8 KHz or 16 KHz. This represents just the *Brian Add* filter. 
\par 
\par See Also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Changing Filters}{\v\f5 Changing_Filters}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Knobs}{\v\f5 Knobs}{\f5 
