\par \pard \qc {\f5 \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Important_Note_About_DC_Antenna_Powers}${\footnote \pard\plain \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Important Note About DC Antenna Powers}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {
\cs16\super K} Important Note About DC Antenna Powers}} {\fs44 Impotant Note about DC Antenna Powers}
\par \pard 
\par {\f5 \tab 
The Bias applied to the TX driver and RX Antenna ports can cause damage to the DSP100 Card if left applied to a short circuit (It is also possible that certain antennas could be damaged). Many antennas are close to zero resistance at DC. Care should be t
aken to ensure that these biases an not on when an antenna of this type is connected to the DSP 100. On some computer, when windows initializes it systematically sets all port locations to one, causing the antenna ports to be turned on. A routine has 
been added to the DSP 100 (RADWARE) installation routine that resets the bias ports to zero. If antennas that have zero DC resistance are connected to the DSP 100 card,  a 0.1uF capacitor in series with the antenna would be a wise additional precaution. A
ll biases are isolated when the Comer Communications Transmitter output module is in use.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb RX DC Antenna Power}{\v\f5 RX_Antenna}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb TX DC Antenna Power}{\v\f5 TX_Antenna}{\f5 
\par 
