 Number Pads}} {\fs44 Number Pads
\par }\pard {\f5 
\par There are several times when are replica of the keyboard number pad is displayed on the screen. These number pads are to allow you to easily enter an exact frequency. It is often easier to get to a known frequency via typing it in than by tuning to it.

\par 
\par   To use the number pad, either click the mouse button on the numbers you wish to enter, or type the frequency on the keyboard. When you have finished, you can click on the appropriate frequency size (Mhz, Khz, or Hz), or press M, K or H. The new frequenc
y will then be shown in the appropriate window.
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} PTT}} {\cs16\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} PTT}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} PTT}} {\fs44 PTT
\par }\pard {\f5 
\par   The PTT control allows the user to change between transmit and receive. In order to be able to transmit, however, two conditions must be met. These condidtions are:
\par   1)  The selected VFO must be tuned to a valid amateur frequency.
\par   2)  You must have entered a valid callsign into the }{\f5\uldb Callsign}{\v\f5 Callsign}{\f5  window.
\par 
\par See Also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Callsign}{\v\f5 Callsign}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Changing PTT}{\v\f5 Changing_PTT}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Turning Knobs}{\v\f5 Turning_Knobs}{\f5 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} RX_Antenna}} {\cs16\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} RX DC Antenna Power}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K}
 RX DC Antenna Power}} {\fs44 RX DC Antenna Power
\par }\pard {\f5 
\par   The RX DC Antenna Power applies +12 Volts DC bias to the RX antenna port. The main application for this bias is when the DSP100 is used with the Comer Communications active antennas. This bias supply can deliver up to 300 mA. 
\par   Please read the }{\f5\uldb Important Note About DC Antenna Powers}{\v\f5 Important_Note_About_DC_Antenna_Powers}{\f5 
\par 
\par See Also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Changing RX DC Antenna Power}{\v\f5 Changing_RX_Antenna}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Important Note About DC Antenna Powers}{\v\f5 Important_Note_About_DC_Antenna_Powers}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Knobs}{\v\f5 Knobs}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb TX DC Antenna Power}{\v\f5 TX_Antenna}{\f5 
\par }
\par \pard \qc \page {\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} Split_Frequency}} {\cs16\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} Split Frequency}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K}
 Split Frequency}} {\fs44 Split Frequency
\par }\pard {\f5 
\par   The split frequency window allows for different frequencies for transmit and receive. To operate split frequency, }{\f5\uldb Tune VFO A}{\v\f5 Changing_VFO_A}{\f5  to one frequency and }{\f5\uldb Tune VFO B}{\v\f5 Changing_VFO_B}{\f5 
 to the second. Then }{\f5\uldb Change the Split Frequency}{\v\f5 Changing_Split}{\f5  knob to the appropriate setting.
\par   The following are the available settings for the split frequency knob:
\par \tab A:\tab Recieve & Transmit on }{\f5\uldb VFO A}{\v\f5 VFO_A}{\f5 .
\par \tab B:\tab Recieve & Transmit on }{\f5\uldb VFO B}{\v\f5 VFO_B}{\f5 .
\par \tab rAxB:\tab Receive on }{\f5\uldb VFO B}{\v\f5 VFO_B}{\f5  & Transmit on }{\f5\uldb VFO B}{\v\f5 VFO_B}{\f5 .
\par \tab rBxA:\tab Receive on }{\f5\uldb VFO B}{\v\f5 VFO_B}{\f5  & Transmit on }{\f5\uldb VFO A}{\v\f5 VFO_A}{\f5 .
\par 
\par See Also:
\par     }{\f5\uldb Changing Split Frequency}{\v\f5 Changing_Split}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Changing VFO A}{\v\f5 Changing_VFO_A}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb Changing VFO B}{\v\f5 Changing_VFO_B}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb VFO B}{\v\f5 VFO_B}{\f5 
\par     }{\f5\uldb VFO B}{\v\f5 VFO_B}{\f5 
\par 
